What You Need
RHEL 9 ISO – Must match your system’s major version (e.g., RHEL 9.2 system → RHEL 9.2 ISO).
Root or sudo access – Needed to install software and edit system files.
ISO file locally – Either copied to your system, or a DVD/USB inserted.
⚙️ Step 1: Create a mount point
Command:
sudo mkdir -p /mnt/rhel_iso
Explanation:
mkdir -p → Makes a directory (folder).
/mnt/rhel_iso → Path where the ISO content will appear. Think of it as opening a folder to see the files inside your ISO.
Check it exists:
ls -ld /mnt/rhel_iso
⚙️ Step 2: Mount the ISO
Case A: ISO is a file on your disk
sudo mount -o loop /path/to/rhel.iso /mnt/rhel_iso
Replace /path/to/rhel.iso with the actual path.
Example:
sudo mount -o loop /home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.2-x86_64-dvd.iso /mnt/rhel_iso
Case B: Using DVD/USB
sudo mount /dev/cdrom /mnt/rhel_iso
Explanation:
-o loop → Treats the ISO file like a physical disk.
After mounting, /mnt/rhel_iso will contain the ISO files, just like opening a USB stick.
Check contents:
ls /mnt/rhel_iso
Expected output:
BaseOS
AppStream
EFI
images
...
BaseOS → Core system packages.
AppStream → Optional applications and extra packages.
⚙️ Step 3: Create a local repo file
Open file for editing:
sudo vi /etc/yum.repos.d/local.repo
(Or use nano if you prefer)
Paste this:
[BaseOS]
name=Local BaseOS Repo
baseurl=file:///mnt/rhel_iso/BaseOS
enabled=1
gpgcheck=0
[AppStream]
name=Local AppStream Repo
baseurl=file:///mnt/rhel_iso/AppStream
enabled=1
gpgcheck=0
Explanation:
[BaseOS] → Repo name.
baseurl=file://... → Path to ISO folders.
enabled=1 → Turns the repo on.
gpgcheck=0 → Skips signature checking (safe for local ISO).
⚙️ Step 4: Disable online repositories
RHEL 9 uses dnf, not yum.
Check enabled repos:
dnf repolist
Disable all repos (except local):
sudo dnf config-manager --disable '*'
sudo dnf config-manager --enable BaseOS AppStream
If config-manager is missing:
sudo dnf install dnf-plugins-core
Manual alternative:
Open any repo file in /etc/yum.repos.d/.
Set enabled=0 for online repos.
⚙️ Step 5: Clean and rebuild cache
sudo dnf clean all
sudo dnf makecache
dnf repolist
Explanation:
Clears old metadata and rebuilds repo info from your ISO.
dnf repolist should now only show BaseOS and AppStream as enabled.
⚙️ Step 6: Test installing a package
Try installing Apache:
sudo dnf install httpd -y
Check if installed:
rpm -q httpd
If it installs, your local repo is working perfectly.
🔁 Optional: Auto-mount ISO on boot
Edit /etc/fstab:
sudo vi /etc/fstab
Add:
/home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.2-x86_64-dvd.iso /mnt/rhel_iso iso9660 loop 0 0
Explanation:
Automatically mounts ISO at /mnt/rhel_iso on system boot.
Test mount without reboot:
sudo mount -a
ls /mnt/rhel_iso

1️⃣ Download the updated ISO
Go to the Red Hat Customer Portal.
Download the latest RHEL 9 DVD ISO (e.g., RHEL 9.3 if you had 9.2 before).
Save it to a location like /home/user/Downloads/.
2️⃣ Replace the old ISO (or mount new ISO)
You have two options:
Option A: Replace ISO file
sudo mv /home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.2-x86_64-dvd.iso /home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.2-backup.iso
sudo mv /home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.3-x86_64-dvd.iso /home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.2-x86_64-dvd.iso
(Keep the same filename if you used /etc/fstab auto-mount.)
Option B: Mount new ISO manually
sudo umount /mnt/rhel_iso
sudo mount -o loop /home/user/Downloads/rhel-9.3-x86_64-dvd.iso /mnt/rhel_iso
3️⃣ Clean DNF cache
After replacing/mounting the new ISO, rebuild the package metadata:
sudo dnf clean all
sudo dnf makecache
dnf repolist
Check: You should see BaseOS and AppStream again.
4️⃣ Update packages from local repo
Now you can update your system with the new ISO:
sudo dnf upgrade -y
This upgrades packages using the new ISO only, without internet.

Step 0: Check existing kernels
uname -r
rpm -q kernel
Example output:
kernel-5.14.0-362.el9.x86_64
kernel-5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64
uname -r → current running kernel
rpm -q kernel → all installed kernels
✅ Keep at least 1 older kernel as a fallback.
1️⃣ Install the new kernel
For example, install the latest available kernel via DNF:
sudo dnf install kernel
This will install a third kernel (say kernel-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64)
Older kernels are kept automatically
/boot will now have three kernels
2️⃣ List all kernels after install
rpm -q kernel
Example output:
kernel-5.14.0-362.el9.x86_64
kernel-5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64
kernel-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64
3️⃣ Set the new kernel as default
Use grubby, which works for both BIOS and UEFI systems:
sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64
Verify the default:
grubby --default-kernel
✅ GRUB will now boot the new kernel by default.
4️⃣ Reboot and verify
sudo reboot
uname -r
Ensure it boots into the new kernel
Older kernels are still present as fallbacks in GRUB menu
5️⃣ Files involved (and what to edit)
RHEL 9 uses BLS (Boot Loader Specification) and auto-generates GRUB configs.
You almost never edit these manually, especially in production.
File Role Edit or not?
/boot/grub2/grub.cfg (BIOS) Main boot config ❌ Do not edit manually
/boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg (UEFI) UEFI boot config ❌ Do not edit manually
/etc/default/grub Default GRUB settings (timeout, cmdline) ✔ Optional edits
/boot/loader/entries/*.conf Per-kernel boot entries ❌ Auto-generated, usually no edits
/boot/vmlinuz-* Kernel image ❌ Don’t delete manually
/boot/initramfs-* Initramfs ❌ Don’t delete manually
✅ Rule of thumb:
Use dnf to install/remove kernels
Use grubby to set default kernel
Avoid manual editing of grub.cfg or bootloader files
6️⃣ Keep older kernels safe
RHEL 9 keeps older kernels unless you remove them.
Optionally, configure a limit of kernels:
sudo vi /etc/dnf/dnf.conf
Add:
installonly_limit=3
This keeps 3 kernels automatically (current + 2 older).
✅ Summary Workflow (Production-safe)
Check current kernels:
uname -r
rpm -q kernel
Install new kernel:
sudo dnf install kernel
Set new kernel as default:
sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-<new-version>
grubby --default-kernel  # verify
Reboot and test:
sudo reboot
uname -r
Keep old kernels for rollback (don’t remove until fully tested).

Safety Principle First
Never remove the kernel you are currently running (uname -r)
Always keep at least 1 fallback kernel
Check GRUB entries before removing
1️⃣ Check which kernel is running
uname -r
Example output:
5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64
✅ Do not remove this kernel
2️⃣ List all installed kernels
rpm -q kernel
Example output:
kernel-5.14.0-362.el9.x86_64
kernel-5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64
kernel-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64   <-- faulty
Identify the faulty kernel (e.g., 5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64)
Confirm at least one older kernel is working
3️⃣ Check GRUB entries (optional but recommended)
sudo grubby --info=ALL
Example snippet:
/boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-362.el9.x86_64
/boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64
/boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64
Confirm faulty kernel is listed
Confirm older working kernels exist
4️⃣ Remove the faulty kernel safely
Use dnf remove with full package name:
sudo dnf remove kernel-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64
dnf will remove:
/boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64
/boot/initramfs-5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64.img
Related modules in /lib/modules/5.14.0-500.el9.x86_64
It will not touch your running kernel
5️⃣ Verify removal
rpm -q kernel
ls /boot/loader/entries/
grubby --info=ALL
The faulty kernel should no longer appear
Older working kernels remain
6️⃣ Optional: Ensure default kernel is correct
If the faulty kernel was set as default, reset it:
sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-427.el9.x86_64
grubby --default-kernel  # verify
7️⃣ Reboot and test
sudo reboot
uname -r
System should boot into default working kernel
GRUB menu shows only working kernels
8️⃣ Optional Cleanup
Remove unused packages and free space:
sudo dnf autoremove
Ensure /boot is tidy

what firewalld does
Controls incoming & outgoing traffic
Uses zones (public, private, trusted, etc.)
Works with iptables / nftables in the background
Allows dynamic changes without restarting the service
📊 Check firewall status
systemctl status firewalld
or
firewall-cmd --state
▶️ Start / Stop firewall
sudo systemctl start firewalld
sudo systemctl stop firewalld
sudo systemctl restart firewalld
Enable at boot:
sudo systemctl enable firewalld

1. What are Firewalld Zones?
A zone is essentially a security profile in firewalld that defines what network traffic is allowed or denied.
Real-world analogy: Think of a zone like a door with a different lock for each type of visitor.
Public Wi-Fi → strict lock (only known visitors allowed)
Home network → more relaxed lock (friends and family allowed)
Trusted network → no restrictions
Technically, a zone is a set of firewall rules applied to one or more network interfaces or IP ranges. Each zone has its own allowed services, ports, and traffic behavior.

# 1. Assign an Interface to a Zone
# Most common method: tie a network interface (e.g., eth0) to a zone

# Syntax
firewall-cmd --zone=<zone> --add-interface=<interface> [--permanent]
firewall-cmd --reload

# Example
# Assign eth0 to the home zone temporarily
firewall-cmd --zone=home --add-interface=eth0

# Make it permanent
firewall-cmd --zone=home --add-interface=eth0 --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Notes
# --permanent → saves the change after reboot
# Without --permanent → the change lasts until the next reboot

# 2. Assign a Source IP or Network to a Zone
# Assign specific IP addresses or entire subnets

# Syntax
firewall-cmd --zone=<zone> --add-source=<IP or subnet> [--permanent]
firewall-cmd --reload

# Example
# Assign IP 192.168.1.50 to the trusted zone
firewall-cmd --zone=trusted --add-source=192.168.1.50 --permanent

# Assign subnet 192.168.1.0/24 to home zone
firewall-cmd --zone=home --add-source=192.168.1.0/24 --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# All traffic from that IP/subnet will use the rules of the assigned zone

# 3. Create a Custom Zone
# If none of the predefined zones fit your needs

# Syntax
firewall-cmd --permanent --new-zone=<zone_name>
firewall-cmd --reload

# Example
firewall-cmd --permanent --new-zone=labzone
firewall-cmd --zone=labzone --add-service=http --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Now labzone behaves like a normal zone, and you can assign interfaces or sources to it

# 4. Check Assigned Zones
# Active zones and their interfaces
firewall-cmd --get-active-zones

# All zones
firewall-cmd --get-zones

# Show services and rules of a specific zone
firewall-cmd --zone=home --list-all

# 1. Block a Specific IP Address
# Syntax
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="<IP>" reject' --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Example: Block IP 192.168.1.100
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.100" reject' --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# ✅ All traffic from that IP is rejected

# 2. Block a Port
# Syntax
firewall-cmd --zone=<zone> --remove-port=<port>/tcp --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Example: Remove SSH port 22 from public zone
firewall-cmd --zone=public --remove-port=22/tcp --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Only blocks incoming traffic to that port
# To block UDP ports similarly, use --remove-port=53/udp

# 3. Block a Service
# Instead of a port, you can block a service by name
firewall-cmd --zone=public --remove-service=http --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Blocks HTTP (port 80) in the public zone

# 4. Using a Zone for Blocking
# Assign an interface or source to the block or drop zone

# Example: Assign a source IP to block zone
firewall-cmd --zone=block --add-source=192.168.1.200 --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# block → rejects incoming connections with ICMP
# drop → silently discards incoming connections

# 5. Block Entire Subnets
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.10.10.0/24" reject' --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Blocks all IPs in the 10.10.10.0/24 network

# 6. Verify Rules
firewall-cmd --list-all
firewall-cmd --zone=block --list-sources
firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports
firewall-cmd --list-rich-rules

# 1. Check Active Ports and Services
# Check all allowed services and ports in a zone
firewall-cmd --zone=<zone> --list-all

# Example:
firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all

# Example Output:
# public (active)
#   interfaces: enp0s3
#   sources: 
#   services: ssh dhcpv6-client
#   ports: 8080/tcp 8443/tcp
#   protocols: 
#   masquerade: no
#   forward-ports: 
#   icmp-blocks: 
#   rich rules: 

# Explanation:
# services → allowed services (SSH, HTTP, etc.)
# ports → open TCP/UDP ports
# interfaces → which network interfaces use this zone

# Check only open ports
firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports

# Check only allowed services
firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-services

# 2. Check Blocked IPs
# Blocked IPs are usually assigned via:
# - rich rules (reject)
# - block/drop zones

# List rich rules
firewall-cmd --list-rich-rules
# Shows IP addresses explicitly blocked with reject or drop

# Check block/drop zone sources
firewall-cmd --zone=block --list-sources
firewall-cmd --zone=drop --list-sources
# Lists IPs or subnets assigned to block/drop zones

# 3. Check Allowed IPs
# Allowed IPs are assigned via:
# - source IPs in trusted/home/work zones
# - interfaces automatically assigned to zones

# List sources for a zone
firewall-cmd --zone=trusted --list-sources
firewall-cmd --zone=home --list-sources
# Shows which IP addresses or subnets are treated as allowed in that zone

# 4. Check Active Zones and Interfaces
firewall-cmd --get-active-zones

# Example Output:
# public
#   interfaces: enp0s3
# trusted
#   sources: 192.168.1.50 192.168.1.0/24
# Shows which interfaces and source IPs are assigned to which zones

# 5. Check All Rules and Status
firewall-cmd --list-all
firewall-cmd --list-all-zones
# --list-all → lists active zone rules
# --list-all-zones → shows rules for all zones (even inactive ones)

# What Are Rich Rules?
# Rich rules are custom firewall rules in firewalld that go beyond the basic “allow service or port” model
# They allow control based on:
# - Source/destination IP
# - Ports and protocols
# - Interfaces
# - ICMP types
# - Logging
# - Actions like accept, reject, drop, or masquerade

# Real-world analogy:
# Normal firewall rule → "Allow SSH on port 22"
# Rich rule → "Allow SSH on port 22 only from 192.168.1.50, log attempts, reject everything else"

# 2. Why Use Rich Rules?
# - Block or allow specific IPs or subnets
# - Allow services only from certain networks
# - Log suspicious traffic
# - Define complex firewall behaviors without editing raw iptables/nftables

# 3. Syntax of Rich Rules
firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=<zone> --add-rich-rule='<rule>'

# General Rule Format:
# rule family="ipv4|ipv6"
#      source address="<IP or subnet>" 
#      destination address="<IP>" 
#      port port="<port>" protocol="tcp|udp" 
#      accept|reject|drop

# Parameters:
# family → IPv4 or IPv6
# source → IP or subnet of sender
# destination → optional, IP of receiver
# port → port number
# protocol → tcp, udp, or icmp
# action → accept, reject, drop

# 4. Examples

# 1️⃣ Block a single IP
firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.100" reject'
firewall-cmd --reload
# Rejects traffic from 192.168.1.100

# 2️⃣ Allow SSH only from a specific IP
firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.50" port port="22" protocol="tcp" accept'
firewall-cmd --reload
# Only 192.168.1.50 can access SSH

# 3️⃣ Log blocked traffic
firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.10.10.0/24" reject log prefix="Blocked IP: "'
firewall-cmd --reload
# Blocks the subnet and logs attempts with prefix "Blocked IP: "

# 4️⃣ Drop traffic from a subnet
firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=drop --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.0.0.0/24" drop'
firewall-cmd --reload
# Silently discards packets from the subnet

# 5. Check Existing Rich Rules
firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-rich-rules
# Displays all rich rules applied to a zone

# What is Masquerading?
# Masquerading is a form of Network Address Translation (NAT) in Linux firewalls

# Real-world analogy:
# - Home network with private IPs (192.168.x.x)
# - All devices access the internet using a single public IP
# - Masquerading hides internal IPs behind the firewall’s public IP
# - Outbound traffic appears to come from the firewall machine, not individual devices

# Technical meaning:
# Masquerading changes the source IP of outgoing packets to the firewall’s IP
# Allows internal networks with private IPs to communicate externally

# 2. When Do You Use Masquerading?
# - Your RHEL machine acts as a router/gateway for a private network
# - Private IP addresses internally (10.0.0.0/24, 192.168.1.0/24)
# - You want them to access the internet via the firewall’s public IP

# Example:
# Private Network: 192.168.1.0/24
# Firewall/Public IP: 203.0.113.5
# Masquerading allows 192.168.1.x devices to access the internet via 203.0.113.5

# 3. How to Enable Masquerading in Firewalld

# Temporary (lost on reboot)
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-masquerade

# Permanent
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-masquerade --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# --zone=external → typically the zone connected to the internet
# Masquerading rewrites source IP addresses of outgoing packets automatically

# 4. Verify Masquerading
firewall-cmd --zone=external --query-masquerade
# Returns 'yes' if enabled, 'no' if not

# Scenario: Home/Office Network Using a RHEL Firewall

# Network Setup:
# Device             IP
# RHEL firewall      203.0.113.5 (public IP facing internet)
# Internal server    192.168.1.10 (private IP)
# Internal desktop   192.168.1.20 (private IP)
# Internal network   192.168.1.0/24

# Internal devices cannot access the internet directly because they have private IPs.
# Goal: Access internet via RHEL firewall, masquerading traffic as 203.0.113.5

# Step 1: Assign Zones
# Assume:
# - Public interface connected to ISP: enp0s3
# - Internal LAN interface: enp0s8

# Public interface (internet)
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-interface=enp0s3 --permanent

# Internal LAN interface
firewall-cmd --zone=internal --add-interface=enp0s8 --permanent

firewall-cmd --reload

# Zones:
# external → zone facing the internet
# internal → zone for private network

# Step 2: Enable Masquerading
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-masquerade --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload
# Outgoing packets from internal network appear as firewall's public IP

# Step 3: Allow Forwarding
# Masquerading requires IP forwarding

# Enable IP forwarding temporarily
echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward

# Make it permanent
sed -i 's/#net.ipv4.ip_forward=1/net.ipv4.ip_forward=1/' /etc/sysctl.conf
sysctl -p

# Step 4: Test
# On internal machine (192.168.1.10):
ping 8.8.8.8
# If working, ping succeeds.
# Internet sees requests as coming from 203.0.113.5

# Check masquerading rules on firewall:
firewall-cmd --zone=external --query-masquerade
# Should return "yes"

# Step 5: Optional – Allow Specific Ports
# Allow HTTP/HTTPS for internal devices
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-service=http --permanent
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-service=https --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Summary – Traffic Flow
# 192.168.1.10/192.168.1.20 (LAN)
#           │
#           ▼
#     enp0s8 (internal interface) [internal zone]
#           │
#           ▼ (masquerading rewrites source IP)
#     enp0s3 (public interface) [external zone] 203.0.113.5
#           │
#           ▼
#         Internet
# Private IPs are hidden
# Firewall handles NAT automatically
# Works for all LAN devices if traffic passes through firewall

# What is Port Forwarding?

# Port forwarding allows:
# - Redirecting incoming traffic from one IP/port to another IP/port inside your network

# Simple Definition:
# - When someone from the internet connects to your public IP + port,
#   the firewall/router forwards that request to a specific internal device and port

# Scenario Setup: Port Forwarding

# Network:
# Device          IP                       Role
# PC1             192.168.1.10             Internal client
# PC2             192.168.1.11             Internal client
# PC3             192.168.1.12             Internal client (hosting web service port 80)
# PC4             192.168.1.13             Internal client
# PC5             192.168.1.14             Internal client
# RHEL Server     192.168.1.1 (LAN), 203.0.113.5 (Public)  Firewall/Router

# Goal:
# Internet users connect to PC3’s web service via 203.0.113.5:8080

# Step 1: Setup the RHEL Firewall Server
# Server interfaces:
# enp0s8 → LAN (192.168.1.1)
# enp0s3 → Public/Internet (203.0.113.5)

# Check interfaces
ip addr show

# Step 2: Assign Zones
# Public interface (Internet)
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-interface=enp0s3 --permanent

# Internal interface (LAN)
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=internal --add-interface=enp0s8 --permanent

sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Step 3: Enable IP Forwarding
# Temporary
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1

# Permanent
sudo sh -c "echo 'net.ipv4.ip_forward=1' >> /etc/sysctl.conf"
sudo sysctl -p

# Step 4: Enable Masquerading (optional if LAN PCs need internet)
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-masquerade --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Step 5: Setup Port Forwarding to PC3
# Forward external 203.0.113.5:8080 → internal 192.168.1.12:80
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=external \
  --add-forward-port=port=8080:proto=tcp:toaddr=192.168.1.12:toport=80

sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Step 6: Allow Traffic on External Port 8080
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Step 7: Test
# From Internet:
curl http://203.0.113.5:8080
# From LAN:
curl http://192.168.1.12

# Step 8: Additional Considerations
# Security: Forward only needed ports
# Multiple PCs: Use different external ports for different internal services
# Example for PC4 SSH on port 2222:
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=external \
  --add-forward-port=port=2222:proto=tcp:toaddr=192.168.1.13:toport=22
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-port=2222/tcp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Logging: Use rich rules to monitor external access if needed

# Step 9: Verify Rules
firewall-cmd --zone=external --list-all
firewall-cmd --zone=external --list-forward-ports

# ✅ Summary of Flow
# Internet
#    │
# 203.0.113.5:8080
#    │ (firewalld port forward)
# RHEL Firewall Server
#    │
# 192.168.1.12:80 (PC3)

# What is ICMP?

# ICMP = Internet Control Message Protocol
# - Network layer protocol
# - Used by devices (computers, routers, servers) to send error messages
#   and operational info about IP packet delivery
# - Unlike TCP/UDP, it does not carry application data; used for control/diagnostics

# Common Uses:
# Function             Example
# Test connectivity    ping 8.8.8.8 → sends Echo Request, receives Echo Reply
# Trace route          traceroute 8.8.8.8 → uses ICMP Time Exceeded messages
# Destination unreachable
#                      Router sends "host unreachable" if packet can't be delivered
# Redirect             Router can tell host to use a different gateway

# ICMP Packet Types:
# - Echo Request / Echo Reply → used by ping
# - Destination Unreachable → network or host unreachable
# - Time Exceeded → TTL expired (used by traceroute)
# - Redirect → tells host to use better route

# Why ICMP Matters in Firewalls:
# - Firewalls may block or allow ICMP
#   If blocked: ping/traceroute fail
#   If allowed: network diagnostics and troubleshooting possible

# Example: Allow ping through firewalld
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-icmp-block-inversion
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-icmp-block=echo-request --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
# echo-request → allows ICMP ping requests through the firewall

